  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nA positive vorticity axis earlier over the Coastal Plains will move \ninland into the Hill Country through this afternoon and tonight; \nfocusing convection / rain chances north of our CWA, leading to a \nlull in our rain chances. The weak cold front will finally push \nthrough late tonight into Saturday morning. \n\nA surface low will likely develop south over the western Gulf, but \nhow far south will greatly determine our rain chances through the \nweekend, lowering my confidence on the future rain coverage. Recent \nguidance has trended to a farther southward progression of the \nsurface low, decreasing rain chances. Nonetheless, overrunning of \nshowers and thunderstorms along with low-level moisture convergence, \nwill lead to a medium to high chance of showers over South Texas. \nGreatest chances and coverage is expected over the Coastal Bend \nthrough Sunday, due to greater convergence and proximity to the low. \n\nThe low washes out as we get a second push of northerly winds \nMonday, lowering PWATs and rain chances. Moisture falls well below \nnormal (PWATs around 1.5\") Tuesday through Thursday due to offshore \nmid-level flow, leading to rain-free conditions.\n\nLastly, due to the persistent northeasterly flow enhancing the Ekman \ntransport mechanism and increasing 7-8 second swell periods, minor \ncoastal flooding is worth monitoring this weekend, especially during \nhigh tide at night into the early morning hours.",
